<h1>@ViewData["Title"]</h1>


<h3>Medicine Expiry & Inventory Alert System </h3>


<h6>1. Introduction</h6>

<p>The Medicine Expiry & Inventory Alert System is designed to help users manage medicine records, monitor stock levels, and track medicine batch expiry dates. This page explains how information is handled within the application.</p>

<h6>2. Information Stored</h6>

<p>The application may store the following inventory information:

- Medicine names, categories, and manufacturers.
- Batch numbers, manufacturing dates, and expiry dates.
- Available stock quantities and minimum stock thresholds.
- Purchase prices, selling prices, and supplier names.

The application is intended for inventory management and does not require patients' personal medical records.</p>

<h6>3. Use of Information</h6>

<p>The stored information is used to:

- Maintain medicine inventory records.
- Identify expired and soon-to-expire batches.
- Monitor low-stock medicines.
- Display inventory summaries and dashboard alerts.
</p>
<h6>4. Data Storage and Security</h6>

<p>Inventory information is stored in the application's configured MySQL database. Access to the database and application should be restricted to authorized users.
</br>
Security depends on the application's configuration, database permissions, and the environment in which it is deployed.</p>

<h6>5. Data Sharing</h6>

<p>The application is not designed to share inventory records with third parties automatically. Any data sharing depends on how the application is configured and operated.</p>

<h6>6. Data Accuracy</h6>

<p>Users are responsible for entering accurate medicine details, batch numbers, stock quantities, and expiry dates. Incorrect information may affect inventory alerts.</p>

<h6>7. Data Retention and Deletion</h6>

<p>Inventory records remain stored until they are deleted or otherwise removed according to the application's database and backup procedures.</p>

<h6>8. Educational Project Disclaimer</h6>

<p>This application is developed as an educational project to demonstrate medicine inventory management and expiry-alert functionality. It should not replace professional pharmacy inventory procedures or applicable regulatory requirements.</p>

<h6>9. Contact</h6>

<p>For questions about this application's privacy practices, contact the person or organization responsible for operating the system.</p>
